### Interpretations
1. [derived from Facts 6, 31, 35, 40, 41, 42, 43, 44, 47] Across the reviewed Experiment 007-009 runs, Narrative appears more often than other stages as the earliest observable stage that departs from the requested company-first editorial hierarchy.
2. [derived from Facts 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 48] The record shows that a company-first Idea can be preserved upstream but downstream stages (Narrative and Compose) can still introduce orchestration-centered framing, unsupported audience/validation claims, or factual amplification.

### Unknowns
1. Whether Narrative-stage changes causally cause downstream hierarchy drift rather than co-occurring with it.
2. Whether interventions targeted at Narrative will, by themselves, reduce the overall incidence of companyproduct hierarchy drift across full-pipeline runs.
3. The specific mechanisms by which Narrative outputs come to reframe company-first inputs (e.g., prompt wording, model behavior, or prompt snapshot format leakage).
4. Whether observed upstream format references in frozen prompts caused any specific run's hierarchy drift.
5. Quantitative prevalence (exact counts, percentages) of runs across a larger corpus beyond the runs explicitly reported where Narrative is the first-visible drift stage.
6. Whether editorial-quality measures (opening pull, readability, memorable phrasing) would correlate with retention of a company-first hierarchy if systematically measured.
7. The representativeness of the reviewed runs for other briefs, teams, or pipeline configurations.
8. Whether Compose-only fixes could, in some runs, fully prevent hierarchy drift when upstream stages remain unchanged.

